## Approach 1 — My Solution
1. Reject `n <= 0` immediately (ugly numbers must be positive).
2. Loop through the factors `{2, 3, 5}`.
3. For each factor, keep dividing `n` while it's divisible.
4. After all divisions, if `n == 1` → ugly, else not.

```cpp
bool isUgly(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) return false;
    for (int factor : {2, 3, 5}) {
        while (n % factor == 0) {
            n /= factor;
        }
    }
    return n == 1;
}
```

## Approach 2 — Alternative
Same logic, just unrolled — no loop, one `while` per factor.

```cpp
bool isUgly(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) return false;
    while (n % 2 == 0) n /= 2;
    while (n % 3 == 0) n /= 3;
    while (n % 5 == 0) n /= 5;
    return n == 1;
}
```

## Complexity
- Time: O(log n) — each division shrinks `n`
- Space: O(1) — no extra memory
